---
/*
  FragmentBrowser — the workshop's browse-and-choose drawer: a large <dialog>
  holding a mini Fragment Manager (the same table/filters as /admin/fragments,
  served by the fragments-panel partial). TWO ROOMS USE IT, and the second one
  is why this file stopped being about constellations:

   · mode="pick"  — the composer's Add surface. Browse the whole corpus, place
     with the per-row ＋ or checkbox-select many and place them together
     (`constellations.place`). Rows already in this constellation render dimmed
     and unselectable. Clicking a row opens its real editor in a sheet on top;
     the header's Add ▾ creates fragments that auto-place.
   · mode="pair"  — the song sheet's pairing picker (plan 39). ONE writing, one
     foreign key (`songs.pair`). No cart, no Add ▾, and no editors at all.

  ⚠ `pick` IS THE BIGGER MODE, NOT THE DEFAULT ONE. Everything `pair` lacks it
  lacks for a stated reason, not by not-yet-being-built — see each region below.
  The instinct while reading this file will be to make the two symmetrical;
  don't. Placing is curation (you must read what you curate, hence the editors),
  pairing is one decision you have already made by the time you open the drawer.

  ⚠ AND EVERYTHING INSIDE IS CLASS-SCOPED. The dialog itself carries the only
  id, because /admin/fragments now mounts BOTH a manage-mode panel and this
  drawer, and two `#fb-panel`s on one page is one `#fb-panel` and a silent bug.
  The panel this wraps was written class-scoped from the start for exactly this
  reason (FragmentListPanel.astro) — the shell simply never followed until now.
  `id` stays a prop so BackToTop can name one instance's scroller.

  Nothing here navigates. A sheet that saves on top of this drawer, and this
  drawer closing after a placement, both announce `fragments:changed` — the host
  refreshes in place and this drawer refreshes its own rows, so composing
  survives a save with its scroll position, its view mode and its unsaved form
  edits intact. The #browse hash is kept for a genuine hard refresh (pick only).
*/
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import TypeMark from './TypeMark.astro';
import BackToTop from '../BackToTop.astro';

interface Props {
  /** Which room this instance serves. See the header. */
  mode?: 'pick' | 'pair';
  /** The dialog's own id — the ONE id in here, so two can coexist on a page. */
  id?: string;
  /** pick only: the target constellation. */
  constellation?: string;
  /** Shown on rows the moment they're placed, so the column stays truthful. */
  constellationName?: string;
  /** The "what is this for" reminder while you pick (docs/admin.md §2). */
  constellationDescription?: string | null;
  /** Colour slot, so a chip added live matches the server-rendered ones. */
  constellationColor?: string | null;
}
const {
  mode = 'pick',
  id = mode === 'pair' ? 'pair-browser' : 'fbrowser',
  constellation,
  constellationName = '',
  constellationDescription,
  constellationColor,
} = Astro.props;
const pick = mode === 'pick';
---

<dialog
  id={id}
  class="fbrowser drawer-dialog w-full max-w-5xl"
  data-mode={mode}
  data-constellation={constellation}
  data-constellation-name={constellationName || undefined}
  data-constellation-color={constellationColor}
  aria-labelledby={`${id}-title`}
>
  <div class="flex h-full flex-col">
    <header class="border-base-300 flex shrink-0 items-center gap-3 border-b px-6 py-3">
      <div class="min-w-0">
        {
          pick ? (
            <h2 id={`${id}-title`} class="font-display truncate text-xl">
              Add to <span class="fb-cname text-primary">{constellationName}</span>
            </h2>
          ) : (
            /* ⚠ THE SONG IS NAMED IN THE HEADING, and it is filled at open time
              rather than rendered: one drawer serves every song the sheet
              opens. "Pair … with a piece" rather than "Add to" because the
              verb is the whole difference between the two modes, and a picker
              that says the wrong verb is how you end up believing you placed
              something in a suite. */
            <h2 id={`${id}-title`} class="font-display truncate text-xl">
              Pair <span class="fb-cname text-primary" /> with a piece
            </h2>
          )
        }
        {
          /* Rendered even when empty, so a description added in the form behind
          this drawer has somewhere to land without a reload (sky:changed). In
          pair mode it is a fixed line rather than a constellation's words. */
        }
        <p
          class="fb-cdesc admin-hint line-clamp-1"
          title={pick ? constellationDescription : undefined}
          hidden={pick && !constellationDescription}
        >
          {pick ? constellationDescription : 'One piece at a time. Anything already holding another song will say so.'}
        </p>
      </div>
      {
        /* ⚠ THE MARKUP IS NOT `SheetError`'S BUT THE SEAM IS (plan 38 · §3).
          That component hard-codes `mx-6 mt-4`, which is the inset for an error
          line at the top of a form panel; this one sits INSIDE the header row
          between the title and the ✕, so it needs no margin and a smaller type
          size, and `class` on that component appends rather than replaces.

          What §3 actually asked for is the half that matters and is honoured
          here: `data-sheet-error`, so `sheetError(root)` finds it BY ROLE and
          this drawer does not mint a twenty-first invented id. The class stays
          only as the thing Tailwind hangs the header layout on. */
      }
      <p class="fb-error admin-alert px-2 py-1 text-xs" data-sheet-error hidden role="alert"></p>
      {
        /* ⚠ NO Add ▾ IN PAIR MODE, and its replacement is the footer bar below.
          This menu makes a fragment *to place in this suite*; the pair picker's
          equivalent question — "the piece I mean does not exist yet" — is
          answered by typing its name in the search field, which is where you
          already are. A menu would ask you to name it twice. */
      }
      {
        pick && (
          <div class="relative ml-auto">
            <button
              class="fb-add-btn btn btn-primary btn-sm gap-1 font-sans"
              aria-haspopup="menu"
              aria-expanded="false"
            >
              <Icon name="ph:plus" /> Add <span aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
            </button>
            <div
              class="fb-add-menu rounded-box border-base-300 bg-base-100 absolute right-0 z-30 mt-1 w-40 overflow-hidden border py-1 shadow-lg"
              hidden
              role="menu"
              aria-label="Add a fragment"
            >
              {/* `w-3` on both marks — the mark is a COLUMN here rather than an
                  inline character, so the two labels keep a common left edge
                  now that writing has no glyph (TYPE_META, 2026-08-17). */}
              <button
                data-new-writing
                role="menuitem"
                class="add-item flex w-full items-center gap-2 px-3 py-2 text-left text-sm"
              >
                <TypeMark type="writing" class="w-3 text-center" /> Writing
              </button>
              <button
                data-new="quote"
                role="menuitem"
                class="add-item flex w-full items-center gap-2 px-3 py-2 text-left text-sm"
              >
                <TypeMark type="quote" class="w-3 text-center" /> Quote
              </button>
              {/* NO SONG, and its absence is the decision rather than an omission
                  (ADR 0031). This menu makes a fragment *to place in this suite*,
                  and a song is never a suite stanza — it accompanies a
                  constellation through `score_url` or through an essay's
                  `paired_song_id`, both relations rather than membership. Offering
                  a create here would make something the picker below cannot show
                  and `constellations.place` refuses. Songs enter at
                  /admin/listening, in the same motion as the listening. */}
            </div>
          </div>
        )
      }
      <button
        type="button"
        data-fb-close
        class:list={['text-base-content/50 hover:text-base-content', !pick && 'ml-auto']}
        aria-label="Close">✕</button
      >
    </header>

    <div class="fb-panel grow overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain px-6 py-4">
      <p class="text-base-content/50 font-sans text-sm">Loading fragments…</p>
    </div>

    <BackToTop scroller={`#${id} .fb-panel`} inDialog class="bottom-20" />

    {
      /* The cart bar — it appears once anything is in the cart, and STAYS while
        you change the filter underneath it. Two controls exist only because of
        that: the "· n shown here" half of the count, which explains a number
        you can't finish counting on screen, and Clear — with a persisting cart,
        "change the filter" is no longer the way to empty it, so there has to be
        one.

        ⚠ PICK ONLY. `paired_song_id` is one key on one row; there is no "pair
        all four of these" to express, so the pair drawer has no cart to show
        and its footer holds a different verb entirely. */
    }
    {
      pick && (
        <footer
          class="fb-bulkbar border-base-300 bg-base-200 flex shrink-0 items-center gap-3 border-t px-6 py-2.5 font-sans text-sm"
          hidden
        >
          <span class="fb-bulkcount text-base-content/70 whitespace-nowrap">0 selected</span>
          <button class="fb-place-selected btn btn-primary btn-xs normal-case">Place in constellation</button>
          <button class="fb-clear-selected btn btn-ghost btn-xs ml-auto normal-case">Clear</button>
        </footer>
      )
    }

    {
      /* ⚠ THE PIECE THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET (plan 39 · rulings 6 and 7), and it
        sits in the footer for two reasons rather than one.

        The reason it is not a row inside the table: the table is a SHARED
        SERVER PARTIAL that knows nothing about this mode, and it is replaced
        wholesale on every filter change — a create row rendered into it would
        have to be re-injected after each swap, and would carry a stale search
        term for the frame in between.

        The reason the footer is right anyway: EntityCombo settled the ordering
        question already — ＋ Add «what you typed» goes AFTER the matches, never
        above them, because putting "make a new one" first offers a duplicate
        before the real thing. A bar below the list is that ordering, and it is
        the same corner of the drawer where pick keeps its own verb.

        It types nothing twice: the search term IS the title. */
    }
    {
      !pick && (
        <footer
          class="fb-createbar border-base-300 bg-base-200 flex shrink-0 items-center gap-3 border-t px-6 py-2.5 font-sans text-sm"
          hidden
        >
          <button class="fb-create btn btn-primary btn-xs gap-1 normal-case">
            <Icon name="ph:plus" /> Write about “<span class="fb-create-term" />”
          </button>
          <span class="admin-hint">Starts a draft with that title and pairs this song to it.</span>
        </footer>
      )
    }
  </div>
</dialog>

{
  /* ⚠ THIS SCRIPT WIRES THE COMPOSER'S DRAWER ONLY, and it self-guards rather
    than being rendered conditionally — an Astro `<script>` inside a `{}`
    expression is inert HTML, not a hoisted module, so a conditional import
    would silently ship a dead drawer. `fragment-browser.ts` looks for a
    pick-mode root and returns if there isn't one.

    The pair drawer is wired by song-sheet.ts instead, because it has to be told
    WHICH song it is open on and that changes every time the sheet loads one.
    A self-wiring module has no way to be told. */
}
<script>
  import '../../scripts/fragment-browser';
</script>
